body:
The ecological consequences of deforestation are severe. When trees are cut down, the soil is exposed to direct rainfall, causing rapid soil erosion and stripping away nutrients. Furthermore, forests act as vital carbon sinks, absorbing carbon dioxide ($$\text{CO}_2$$
) through photosynthesis. Removing trees reduces this carbon-absorbing capacity, allowing more $$\text{CO}_2$$
 to accumulate in the atmosphere and accelerating global warming.
